      "evidence_id": "e-0283ad633d0201822409",
      "text": "Kimolos\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\t\t\n\n\t\t\t\t\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\nKimolos – Kimolis – Echinousa – Argentara – Argentiera – Argentière – Cimolus – Kimilos – Kimoulo\n\nFrom Ios towards the west Sikinos and Lagousa and Folegandros […] and close to these is Kimolos, whence the earth of Kimolia …\n\nStrabo Geographica 10. 5. 1\n\nKimolia is an island, which was also called Kimolis, and Kimolos, and commonly Kimolos, and by the Italians Argintierra, whose soils are full of nitron. It is 20 miles in circumference, being nearest to Milos, and to Polyaigos.\n\nMeletios Geographica 18\n\n \n\nKimolos is the ancient name of the island, as handed down by ancient and modern writers and geographers. Western cartographers called it Argentiera, Argyri or the Silver Island, from the silver colour of its coasts or from the silver mines that they believed it had.\n\nIn its key geographical position on the course of maritime routes, Kimolos forms, together with Milos and Polyaigos, an island cluster at the southwestern entrance of the Archipelago. Its small size and the little distance separating it from Milos seem to have determined its development over time, as well as the relations between the two neighbouring islands.\n\nArid, with a rocky, steep coastline in the north and a little low-lying land mainly in the southern and southwestern parts, it belongs to the volcanic arc of the southern Aegean and is mostly covered by various volcanic rocks.\n\nIts geological structure had already acquired economic importance in antiquity: the famous ‘Kimolian earth’, the mineral white clay named after the island, has been used since ancient times in pharmaceutical and cosmetic preparations, but also for cleaning the body and clothing. It was a main export product.",
